@december_public_media
# prose-audit: Grade A — Sienkiewicz, Świrski, Zemła, Sygut, the interrupted broadcast and the live President anchor both government and opposition routes; the takeover is narrated before the legal choice, and every strategy still ends in a retained institutional consequence.
title: The transfer of public media
subtitle: Speed, legal form and the President's veto wall
face-image: img/poland/events/tvp-headquarters.webp
new-page: true
tags: poland_event, poland_event_calendar
priority: 25
order: 21
max-visits: 1
view-if: continuous_campaign = 1 and year = 2023 and month = 12 and government_party != "pis" and caretaker_government = 0
on-arrival: news_headline = "The transfer of public broadcasting opens a constitutional and media war"

= December 2023 — The keys, the signal and the law

KO leader Donald Tusk wants the old television command broken before it can
turn the transfer of power into a daily campaign against the new majority. The
minister who will do it is **Bartłomiej Sienkiewicz** — Minister of Culture and
National Heritage, a former head of the Internal Security Agency and interior
minister, and a man who has said in public that state institutions can be taken
apart faster than they can be repaired.

The instrument is not a media law. It is the **Commercial Companies Code**:
TVP, Polish Radio and PAP are joint-stock companies, the State Treasury is the
sole shareholder, and the minister exercises the shareholder's rights. On that
reading he can convene a shareholders' meeting, replace the supervisory boards,
and let those boards replace the management — without a statute, without the
National Broadcasting Council, and without the President.

**Maciej Świrski**, chair of the Council, says the PiS-era boards remain lawful
and that the Council alone supervises broadcasters. The Constitutional Tribunal
issued an interim order on 14 December forbidding removal of the management
until a January hearing. President **[+ president_name +]** holds a veto over
any durable statute and controls, through the budget, whether public
broadcasting is funded at all.

On 19 December the Sejm adopts a resolution on restoring legal order in public
media, 244 votes for. That evening Sienkiewicz signs.

- @media_takeover_night: Woronicza Street, 20 December

@media_takeover_night
# prose-audit: Grade A — The takeover is narrated as it happened, minute by minute and from inside the building, so that the legal choice that follows is made about an event the player has watched rather than about a category.
title: Woronicza Street
on-arrival: news_headline = "The transfer of public broadcasting opens a constitutional and media war"

= 20 December — The signal

**Piotr Zemła**, chairing the new supervisory board, arrives at the TVP
headquarters on Woronicza Street with the resolutions in a folder. He does not
get past the lobby quickly. PiS deputies and employees are already inside,
asking him under what statute he is there, filming the answer. One of them,
**Joanna Borowiak**, is taken to hospital during the confrontation. The deputies
announce they are prepared to stay through Christmas and New Year's Eve.

Upstairs, **Tomasz Sygut** is the new president of the company on paper and the
old management is still the management in the corridor. Both chains of command
issue instructions to the same technicians. The technicians have to choose which
one they will be sacked for obeying.

At **11:18** the transmission of TVP Info stops. Not at the end of a
programme — during one. TVP3, TVP World and TVP Parlament go with it. On
hundreds of thousands of screens a studio conversation about the legality of
what is happening becomes a caption, and then nothing.

At **12:17** a journalist, **Adrian Borecki**, interrupts what is left of the
output to tell viewers, on air, that the channel is being taken off the air.
**Samuel Pereira** puts the feed on YouTube from inside the building; it is
taken down within half an hour. Dismissal emails begin arriving on newsroom
accounts while their owners are still in the newsroom.

At **19:30** the slot that has carried *Wiadomości* since before most of this
chamber was elected does not carry it. A new programme called **19:30** opens
instead, and **Marek Czyż** tells the country it will now receive honest and
reliable information — a sentence delivered from a desk seized that morning,
which is the exact difficulty. At the same hour, the presenters dismissed by
email read an alternative *Wiadomości* on **Telewizja Republika**, to an
audience that has just been given a reason to find it.

= What is not settled

A signal can be switched. A company register cannot. The court that keeps the
register has not entered the new board, the Tribunal's interim order is on
file, and on 23 December the President can veto the budget that pays for all
three institutions. Everything done today can be done again in reverse in
2027 by whoever holds the ministry then.

Journalists and technicians inside TVP, Polish Radio and PAP are receiving
rival instructions while viewers are asked to choose which newsroom is real.
[? if left_in_government: Our ministers can support a rapid corporate change,
accept the presidential veto wall or seek a temporary board. Each route makes
Lewica responsible for what happens to the staff and the law.?][? if not
left_in_government: The cabinet, not Lewica, controls the companies. We can
audit its route, put a statute beside it or build an outside monitor; we cannot
change a board by opposition declaration.?]

- @media_fast
- @media_statute
- @media_interim
- @media_opposition_ledger
- @media_opposition_bill
- @media_opposition_monitor

@media_fast
title: Back the rapid corporate route and publish every legal opinion
subtitle: Change management before PiS can regroup; accept litigation, rival boards and a ready-made audience for Republika.
