Sienkiewicz finishes what he began on Woronicza Street: new managers hold the
buildings under corporate resolutions, and the signal changes before the old
boards accept dismissal. When the register court and the Tribunal refuse to
recognise the new boards in January, the ministry answers with liquidation —
a shareholder's power the President's budget veto has just made plausible.
Staff can return to work under one newsroom, but courts and rival regulators
now hold competing claims to the same institution.

PiS loses its broadcaster and gains a story about seizure. Presenters and
viewers move together toward Republika; speed has settled control for tonight,
not legality for the next government.

