= December 2023 — A list only one party wrote

The Chancellery sends every coalition partner a working document. It is not
the coalition agreement, which runs to thirteen mostly institutional points
and which every leader actually signed. It is **100 konkretów na pierwsze 100
dni** — the list **Donald Tusk**, Prime Minister and leader of Koalicja
Obywatelska, read out through the summer campaign — reformatted with two new
columns: responsible ministry, and date.

[? if hundred_promises_2023_done = 1: We watched him announce it in June, and
it was a good list to run on. The sixty-thousand-złoty tax-free allowance,
the morning-after pill without a prescription, in-vitro funding, the abortion
law, cheap housing credit, higher teachers' pay, and the accounting for eight
years of PiS. Nobody asked at the time which party would be carrying which
line, because at the time the question was whether any of us would be carrying
anything at all.?][? if hundred_promises_2023_done = 0: It is a campaign
document. This is the first time most of the coalition has been asked to read
it as an instruction.?]

**Krzysztof Paszyk**, chair of the PSL parliamentary club, says the quiet part
into a microphone within a day: *nas obowiązuje umowa, a nie konkrety
Platformy*. Third Way campaigned on its own twelve guarantees and considers
that settled. Poland 2050 splits publicly — **Barbara Oliwiecka** calls the
hundred konkrety KO's *autorskie propozycje*, **Ryszard Petru** says the whole
coalition should own them and be judged on them.

The trap in the document is arithmetical rather than ideological. About a
dozen items are ours in everything but the letterhead, and Third Way will not
pass them. Two of the most expensive are KO's alone, and we think they are
regressive. Whoever signs the whole list signs for the parts that cannot pass;
whoever refuses becomes, in March, the reason the list did not happen.

[? if left_in_government = 1: Our ministries appear in the responsible column.
The dates beside them were written by somebody else.?]
[? if left_in_government = 0: We are not in the cabinet, so nothing is assigned
to us. What is wanted is our votes on the days the list needs them, and our
silence on the days it does not.?]
[? if left_holds_marshal = 1: We hold the Marshal's chair, which means the
column headed *date* is in practice ours to write — and that every delay after
today can be addressed to us.?]
[? if kpo_bill_quality >= 2: Konkret nine, the unblocking of the European
money, is already most of the way done and was done in January by a bill we
priced. Nothing in the document mentions this.?]
[? if pension_defence_credit >= 4: We answered the referendum in October on
the social floor rather than refusing it, which is the only reason anybody in
this coalition believes we have a costed position on pensions at all.?]
[? if campaign_march_broke_host_discipline = 1: KO's staff have not forgotten
that our deputies broke the host list's discipline at the march. The word used
about our signature, off the record, is *conditional*.?]

**Anna Maria Żukowska**, the Left's parliamentary club chair, wants an answer
before the first briefing, because whatever we say in the next two days is the
answer we will be held to in March.

- @konkrety_cosign
- @konkrety_annex
- @konkrety_price
- @konkrety_calendar
- @konkrety_refuse

@konkrety_cosign
title: Adopt the hundred konkrety as the government's programme
subtitle: Buys the warmest possible start with KO and a share of the credit; every undelivered line in March is then also ours.
on-arrival: {!
Q.konkrety_line = "Co-signed the hundred konkrety";
Q.konkrety_ownership = 70;
Q.ko_relation += 8;
Q.government_delivery += 2;
Q.public_trust += 2;
Q.coalition_blur += 4;
Q.movement_autonomy = Math.max(0, (Number(Q.movement_autonomy) || 47) - 5);
Q.progressive_credibility = Math.max(
    0, (Number(Q.progressive_credibility) || 0) - 2
);
Q.progressives_dissent += 4;
Q.left_poll_momentum += 0.1;
Q.news_headline =
    "The Left adopts KO's hundred konkrety as the government's own programme";
!}

We answer that a governing coalition with four programmes has no programme,
and that the list is now the government's. The Chancellery gets what it wanted
before Christmas, the briefings for the first weeks write themselves, and our
ministers are inside the delivery machine rather than beside it.

[? if left_in_government = 1: The dates beside our portfolios stand as
written.?][? if left_in_government = 0: We are not in the cabinet and have
just promised our votes for a hundred items we did not draft.?]

What we have bought is a share of the credit. What we have also bought,
without anyone saying so, is a share of every line that PSL will not pass, and
of the sixty-thousand allowance that the finance ministry cannot fund.

- @poland_event_queue: Adopt the list

@konkrety_annex
title: Sign the coalition agreement only, and annex what we will actually carry
subtitle: Cost: 1 resource. Puts our items and their owners in writing now, so that in March the argument is about a document rather than about tone.
choose-if: resources >= 1
unavailable-subtitle: Drafting a costed annex against a hundred-item list needs staff we do not have free this month.
on-arrival: {!
Q.resources -= 1;
Q.konkrety_line = "Coalition agreement plus a written annex";
Q.konkrety_ownership = 40;
Q.konkrety_receipts = 1;
Q.konkrety_left_items = 3;
Q.negotiation_capital += 2;
Q.institutional_trust += 2;
Q.public_trust += 1;
Q.ko_relation -= 2;
Q.government_coalition_dissent += 2;
Q.news_headline =
    "Lewica answers the hundred konkrety with a signed annex of its own items";
!}

Our reply is four pages long and deliberately boring. The binding document is
the coalition agreement. Beside it we attach a list of the konkrety Lewica will
carry, each with the ministry that owns it, the legal instrument it needs and
the month it can realistically reach the chamber. Everything else on KO's list
we will support and will not sponsor.

Paszyk reads it and files an almost identical annex for Third Way within the
week, which is the first thing this coalition has coordinated. The Chancellery
is irritated: a document that says who owns what is a document that can be
checked.

Nobody outside the building notices any of this in December. It will be worth
having in March, when somebody needs to prove what was promised by whom.

- @poland_event_queue: File the annex

@konkrety_price
title: Charge for the signature: three Lewica konkrety, with dates, in the minutes
subtitle: Requires a seat in the cabinet and 1 resource. Trades co-ownership of KO's list for a coalition-council deadline on our own bills.
choose-if: left_in_government = 1 and resources >= 1
unavailable-subtitle: [? if left_in_government = 0: We are not in the cabinet, so there is no coalition council in which our price can be minuted.?][? if left_in_government = 1: There is no negotiating capacity free to convert a signature into a dated commitment.?]
on-arrival: {!
Q.resources -= 1;
Q.konkrety_line = "Signature sold for three dated items";
Q.konkrety_ownership = 55;
Q.konkrety_receipts = 1;
Q.konkrety_left_items = 3;
Q.abortion_cabinet_deadline = 1;
Q.negotiation_capital += 3;
Q.ko_relation += 3;
Q.psl_relation -= 4;
Q.psl_coalition_dissent += 5;
Q.government_coalition_dissent += 3;
Q.feminist_trust += 4;
Q.progressive_credibility += 3;
Q.institutional_trust += 2;
Q.news_headline =
    "The Left signs the government programme for a dated commitment on its own bills";
!}

We sign — and the price is written into the coalition council minutes rather
than into a press release. Three items carry our name and a month: the widow's
pension, recognition for couples the state currently refuses to see, and a
date by which the abortion bills reach a first reading.

Tusk takes the trade because it costs him nothing in December. Paszyk does not
take it at all, and says so; PSL's deputies were not consulted about a calendar
that now exists whether they signed it or not.

A minuted date is not a law. It is, however, the one thing that can be quoted
back at a Prime Minister in front of cameras, and we have made sure it exists
before anybody needs it.

- @poland_event_queue: Minute the price

@konkrety_calendar
title: Use the Marshal's chair and publish the whole list as an order paper
subtitle: Requires the Marshal's chair. Converts a hundred press promises into scheduled votes, so every partner is seen deciding rather than delaying.
choose-if: left_holds_marshal = 1
unavailable-subtitle: The order paper belongs to another party's Marshal; we can complain about the calendar but we cannot write it.
on-arrival: {!
Q.konkrety_line = "Scheduled the list from the Marshal's chair";
Q.konkrety_ownership = 45;
Q.konkrety_receipts = 1;
Q.konkrety_scoreboard = 1;
Q.konkrety_left_items = 2;
Q.parliament_visibility += 6;
Q.institutional_trust += 4;
Q.public_trust += 3;
Q.media_capacity += 3;
Q.ko_relation -= 4;
Q.psl_relation -= 6;
Q.psl_coalition_dissent += 7;
Q.government_coalition_dissent += 6;
Q.news_headline =
    "The Marshal turns the hundred konkrety into a published parliamentary calendar";
!}

We do not argue about whether the list binds anybody. We schedule it. The
Marshal's office publishes an indicative order paper carrying every konkret
that needs a statute, in tranches, from January onward, with the sponsoring
ministry printed beside each one.

This is not generosity. A promise that has been given a sitting day cannot be
described as *in progress*; it is either voted on or visibly pulled, and the
deputy who pulls it does so with their name attached. PSL understands the
mechanism immediately and is furious about it.

The Chancellery discovers that it has lost control of its own campaign
document to the one office that can put things in a queue.

- @poland_event_queue: Publish the order paper

@konkrety_refuse
title: Say publicly that this is not our programme
subtitle: The cleanest thing to say and the most expensive. KO gains a named partner to blame in March; the movement gains a party that did not pretend.
on-arrival: {!
Q.konkrety_line = "Publicly refused the list";
Q.konkrety_ownership = 10;
Q.movement_autonomy += 6;
Q.progressive_credibility += 4;
Q.media_capacity += 2;
Q.ko_relation -= 7;
Q.government_coalition_dissent += 6;
Q.coalition_blur = Math.max(0, (Number(Q.coalition_blur) || 8) - 4);
Q.public_trust -= 2;
Q.party_unity -= 2;
if (Q.razem_in_left) {
    Q.progressive_credibility += 3;
    Q.movement_autonomy += 3;
    Q.left_poll_momentum += 0.1;
}
Q.news_headline = Q.razem_in_left
    ? "Zawisza: this is not our programme and we have nothing to do with it"
    : "The Left refuses to adopt KO's campaign list as a government programme";
!}

[? if razem_in_left = 1: **Marcelina Zawisza** does it in one sentence on
camera — *to nie jest nasz program, nie mamy z tym nic wspólnego* — and the
clip travels further than any of our December policy announcements. Razem's
deputies have been waiting to say this since the campaign.?]
[? if razem_in_left = 0: We say it in a written statement, which is duller and
no less final. The document binding this coalition is the agreement. The
hundred konkrety are one party's manifesto and will be treated as such.?]

It is true, it is popular with everyone who wanted a Left that does not
dissolve into a coalition, and it hands KO exactly what its press operation
will need in eleven weeks: a partner who said, on the record, in December, that
the programme was not theirs.

[? if left_in_government = 1: We are inside the cabinet that is now not
delivering a programme we declined to adopt, which is a position that requires
explaining twice a day.?]

- @poland_event_queue: Let the refusal stand

# Research:
# https://www.gazetaprawna.pl/wiadomosci/kraj/artykuly/11245463,ziobro-fundusz-sprawiedliwosci-sledztwo-najwazniejsze-wydarzenia.html
@suwerenna_walkout_2023
# prose-audit: Grade A — The walkout is counted in seats before it is described, the discomfort is stated as our own people's rather than attributed to commentators, and every route is something a junior partner can actually do to a contract it has just signed.
title: Ziobro's people will not sit in this
subtitle: Solidarna Polska leaves the United Right the day the cross-bloc contract is signed, and takes the government's right flank with it
face-image: img/poland/events/zbigniew-ziobro-2015.webp
new-page: true
tags: poland_event, poland_event_calendar
priority: 46
order: 3
max-visits: 1
view-if: continuous_campaign = 1 and year = 2023 and month = 12 and government_party = "pis" and formation_pis_player_coalition = 1 and formation_solpol_exit_triggered = 1 and suwerenna_walkout_2023_done = 0
on-arrival: {!
Q.suwerenna_walkout_2023_done = 1;
const walkoutSeats = Math.max(0, Math.round(Number(Q.suwerenna_seats) || 0));
Q.culture_walkout_seats = walkoutSeats;
// PiS has just lost the flank that proves it is not going soft, in exchange
// for us. Everything it does to its own base from now on has to be done with
// the instruments the contract left it: schools, culture, family policy.
Q.culture_current_pressure = 24 +
    Math.round(walkoutSeats * 0.8) +
    6 * Math.max(0, 3 - Math.round(Number(Q.formation_pis_left_safeguards) || 0));
Q.culture_current_standing = 44 +
    3 * Math.max(0, 3 - Math.round(Number(Q.formation_pis_left_safeguards) || 0));
Q.razem_dissent += 14;
Q.progressives_dissent += 12;
Q.spring_dissent += 8;
Q.pps_dissent += 10;
Q.coalition_blur += 8;
Q.far_right_agenda += 3;
Q.public_mood_pending_rule_of_law_salience += 10;
Q.news_headline =
    "Solidarna Polska walks out rather than govern beside the Left";
!}

= December 2023 — Two parties leave a room they were both promised

**Zbigniew Ziobro** does not sign. Solidarna Polska — Justice Minister,
Prosecutor General, the prosecution service, the Justice Fund and eight years
of the hardest edge the United Right had — announces that a coalition with the
post-communist left is not a coalition it recognises, and leaves with **[+
culture_walkout_seats +] deputies**. **Patryk Jaki**, **Michał Wójcik** and
**Marcin Warchoł** go with him. They keep the club, the name and the argument
that they are the only ones who never sold anything.

That is the arithmetic. The rest is ours to say out loud, because nobody else
in this building will say it for us.

For a great many of our own people this arrangement was already unbearable
before Ziobro made it look like a victory. The party that spent four years
calling this government a threat to the constitution has signed a contract with
it. Razem's council has published a dissent. The Wiosna deputies who came in on
a secular programme have been asked, repeatedly, by their own constituency
organisations, to explain what the [+ formation_pis_left_safeguards +]
[? if formation_pis_left_safeguards = 1: safeguard is?][? if formation_pis_left_safeguards != 1: safeguards are?]
worth against the pictures. The honest answer is that they are worth exactly
what we are able to enforce, and that we will find out.

And it leaves **[+ prime_minister +]** with a problem that will outlast the
signing photographs. PiS has bought a majority by giving away the flank that
proved it had not gone soft. It now has to demonstrate to its own electorate
that nothing has changed — using only the instruments this contract left it.
Not the prosecutors, which we have written rules around. Schools, culture,
family policy and the people who run them.

There is a queue for that job. **Przemysław Czarnek** is at the front of it.

- @walkout_publish
- @walkout_close_door
- @walkout_members
- @walkout_attack

@walkout_publish
title: Publish the contract and make the safeguards the story
subtitle: Cost: 1 resource. Put the signed text where every journalist and every member can read it against what the cabinet actually does.
choose-if: resources >= 1
unavailable-subtitle: There is no capacity to print, brief and defend the full text this week.
on-arrival: resources -= 1; czarnek_line = "Published contract"; culture_current_pressure -= 6; constitutional_restraint += 4; institutional_trust += 3; public_trust += 3; coalition_blur -= 6; razem_dissent -= 5; progressives_dissent -= 4; pis_relation -= 3; news_headline = "The Left publishes the full cross-bloc contract on the day it is signed"

We put the whole text out, clauses and annexes, before anybody can characterise
it for us. Every safeguard we won is on the page with a number, and so is every
thing we did not get, which is the part that makes it credible.

It does not make the photographs better. It means that when this cabinet does
something our members cannot forgive, the argument will be about a clause and a
date rather than about whether we are the kind of people who would.

- @poland_event_queue: Publish the text

@walkout_close_door
title: Demand a written clause that Ziobro's people cannot be taken back
subtitle: Cost: 1 resource. The exclusivity we should have signed at formation, bought now while PiS still needs the photograph.
choose-if: resources >= 1
unavailable-subtitle: The negotiating team has nothing left to spend this week.
on-arrival: resources -= 1; czarnek_line = "No-return clause"; united_right_return_blocked = 1; culture_current_pressure -= 4; culture_current_standing -= 4; coalition_blur -= 3; pis_relation -= 5; party_unity += 3; far_right_agenda -= 3; news_headline = "The contract acquires a clause forbidding a return of the walkout deputies"

We ask for the only thing that makes this coalition survivable: that the
deputies who walked out today cannot be readmitted to the governing majority
without our consent. PiS objects that it does not discipline its own former
allies at somebody else's request, and signs, because the alternative headline
on the first day is worse.

It is a piece of paper against a bloc that has existed since 2005. It is also
the difference between a partner that has an alternative and a partner that
would have to build one in public.

- @poland_event_queue: Get the clause signed

@walkout_members
title: Take the argument to our own members before anybody else does
subtitle: Spend the week inside the party rather than on television; the coalition gains nothing and the party might survive it.
on-arrival: czarnek_line = "Internal settlement"; razem_dissent -= 10; progressives_dissent -= 9; spring_dissent -= 6; pps_dissent -= 7; party_unity += 6; movement_autonomy += 4; media_capacity -= 2; left_poll_momentum -= 0.15; news_headline = "The Left's leadership spends the signing week in its own regional halls"

No studio appearances. Regional meetings, in halls, with the text and the
arithmetic and the question taken from anyone who wants to ask it. The answer
given is the true one: that this was a choice between a government we can put a
floor under and one we cannot, that it may well be wrong, and that the people in
this room will be the ones who decide whether it was.

We lose a week of national coverage to Ziobro, who uses it. The party that comes
out of those halls is one organisation rather than three, which is the resource
every subsequent month of this will be paid out of.

- @poland_event_queue: Go to the regional halls

@walkout_attack
title: Turn on the walkout: make Ziobro's record the government's first act
subtitle: The easiest week available. It also tells PiS's base that its new partner intends to prosecute its old one.
on-arrival: czarnek_line = "Attack the walkout"; culture_current_pressure += 10; culture_current_standing += 6; media_capacity += 5; left_poll_momentum += 0.3; justice_fund_salience += 8; pis_relation -= 4; far_right_agenda += 3; razem_dissent -= 3; institutional_trust -= 2; news_headline = "The Left opens the coalition by putting the Justice Fund on the front page"

The Justice Fund, the grants, the surveillance billing, the eight years of the
prosecution service run as a political instrument — all of it, from a government
podium, on day one. It is true, it is popular, and it is the only part of this
week that our own members enjoy.

It also lands on PiS's electorate as the sound of their party helping the left
prosecute their own. Every commentator on that side draws the same conclusion
within a fortnight: the leadership has traded the right flank for us, and will
have to pay the base back somewhere the contract does not reach.

- @poland_event_queue: Put the fund on the front page
